[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Activity and Fragment Lifecycle Internals in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the internals of Android Activity and Fragment lifecycles is critical for building robust, memory-leak-free applications. This tutorial explores the state machines, callback sequences, and how Fragments hook into their parent Activities."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Activity Lifecycle State Machine"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An Activity transitions through several states managed by the ActivityManagerService (AMS) and the application's main thread. The core lifecycle methods represent transitions between these states."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onCreate(): Called when the activity is first created. Ideal for instantiating UI, binding data to lists, and setting up ViewModels."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onStart(): Makes the activity visible to the user as the app prepares for the activity to enter the foreground and become interactive."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onResume(): The activity is now in the foreground. This is where you should initialize components that need to run while the activity is visible and focused."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onPause(): Called when the system is about to resume a previous activity. Used to release heavy CPU resources or animations."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onStop(): The activity is no longer visible. Heavy-duty shutdown operations, such as writing data to a database, should happen here."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onDestroy(): The final callback received before the activity is destroyed. Always clean up persistent threads and pending asynchronous tasks here to prevent memory leaks."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Logging in an Activity"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To observe the lifecycle sequence firsthand, override the primary lifecycle methods in your Java Activity class and add Logcat statements."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class LifecycleActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private static final String TAG = "LifecycleActivity";

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_lifecycle);
        Log.d(TAG, "onCreate: Called");
    }

    @Override
    protected void onStart() {
        super.onStart();
        Log.d(TAG, "onStart: Called");
    }

    @Override
    protected void onResume() {
        super.onResume();
        Log.d(TAG, "onResume: Called");
    }

    @Override
    protected void onPause() {
        super.onPause();
        Log.d(TAG, "onPause: Called");
    }

    @Override
    protected void onStop() {
        super.onStop();
        Log.d(TAG, "onStop: Called");
    }

    @Override
    protected void onDestroy() {
        super.onDestroy();
        Log.d(TAG, "onDestroy: Called");
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Fragment Lifecycle Internals"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Fragments are hosted inside Activities and have a tightly coupled lifecycle that mimics the host Activity while introducing distinct View-specific callbacks. A common source of bugs is failing to differentiate between the Fragment lifecycle and its associated View lifecycle."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onAttach(): Called when the fragment is first associated with its context (Activity)."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onCreateView(): Inflates the Fragment user interface layout."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onViewCreated(): Called immediately after onCreateView() returns, ideal for setting up View bindings and RecyclerView adapters."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onDestroyView(): Cleans up references to the View to avoid severe memory leaks inside the Fragment."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onDetach(): Called when the fragment is disassociated from the host activity."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Implementing a Fragment Lifecycle Observer"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a properly structured Fragment in Java, managing its View lifecycle cleanly."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"public class SampleFragment extends Fragment {
    private static final String TAG = "SampleFragment";
    private FragmentSampleBinding binding;

    @Override
    public void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        Log.d(TAG, "onCreate: Fragment initialized");
    }

    @Override
    public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                             Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        binding = FragmentSampleBinding.inflate(inflater, container, false);
        Log.d(TAG, "onCreateView: View inflated");
        return binding.getRoot();
    }

    @Override
    public void onViewCreated(@NonNull View view, Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState);
        Log.d(TAG, "onViewCreated: Setup views here");
    }

    @Override
    public void onDestroyView() {
        super.onDestroyView();
        binding = null;
        Log.d(TAG, "onDestroyView: Nullified binding to prevent leaks");
    }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Best Practices"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always nullify Fragment View bindings inside onDestroyView() to prevent retaining references to destroyed views."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid performing long-running network or disk operations inside onResume() or onPause() to keep the UI thread unblocked."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Android Architecture Components such as ViewModel and LiveData to survive configuration changes like screen rotations automatically."}]
